# Docker 29 exited-network compatibility

Actual `runtime-03/stopped-container-inspect.json` records container `406ac450c875815b04db196d7e3361b52451262ee3c16151665238461a1b3d9d` exited with code 3, PID 0 and no running process. Its none-network entry retains NetworkID `69de2cf16fd91b58115a6f4fa3cde31546ca5fe26c84d0a3fc438b47cb0cd5ad` while EndpointID, SandboxID, and SandboxKey are empty. The validator rejected this because it required NetworkID and EndpointID to be simultaneously populated. All addresses, routes, ports, aliases, and DNS authority remain empty in the actual inspection.

Changed only `core/pit_optimizer_v5/production_sandbox.py` source. The closed network parser now permits a retained valid NetworkID with empty EndpointID only when both sandbox fields are empty. Lifecycle validation classifies precisely this representation as an empty namespace only for exited containers. Created and running containers still reject it. The retained ID remains in the network attestation; it is not discarded. Exactly the none-network attachment and all existing closed-field, address, routing, identity, mount, image, and resource checks remain required. Existing private-to-empty exited transitions and the previously reviewed cleanup recovery can therefore authenticate this teardown without adopting a live endpoint or namespace.

Source SHA256: `8912da187371efd6e01b7fd5a25775ba880dd53ad0db768bed59e0aafbdb8d7d`.

Scoped Ruff, in-memory compile without application imports, and diff whitespace verification passed. No tests, probes, application/Docker/network execution, runtime artifact edits, staging, hook changes, or commits. The source and this report are the only written paths for this increment. Actual cleanup is controller-owned; the failed evaluation remains frozen and is not performance evidence.
